# =============================================================================
# HTTPS ON THE SAUNA'S OWN NETWORK.
#
# WHAT THIS SOLVES
#   Browsers increasingly treat plain http:// as broken — warnings in the bar,
#   and some features (a card reader talking to the page, "save this password")
#   simply refuse to work without a padlock. But a certificate from Let's
#   Encrypt needs a public address, and this box deliberately has none.
#
#   So Caddy runs its own small certificate authority, issues a certificate to
#   a name that only exists inside the building, and each terminal is told once
#   to trust that authority. `tls internal` is the whole of that arrangement.
#
# THE TWO THINGS THAT MAKE IT WORK, both done by hand once per terminal:
#   1. Point the name at this box. Mississauga has no router that can host DNS
#      entries, so add a line to each terminal's own hosts file:
#           <this box's IP address>   pos-mississauga.local
#      Windows: C:\Windows\System32\drivers\etc\hosts, edited as administrator.
#      Two devices — the front desk terminal and the kitchen display.
#   2. Trust the certificate authority. Export Caddy's root certificate from
#      the caddy_data volume and install it on both terminals. Without this,
#      every page load shows a security warning and staff learn to click
#      through warnings, which is a worse habit than plain http.
#
# WHY THIS FILE IS SHORTER THAN THE ORIGINAL GUIDE'S VERSION
#   That version had Caddy serving the built client files itself, from
#   /srv/client, with separate rules for /api and /socket.io. It doesn't need
#   to any more: the server already serves the client under one origin (the
#   "three problems solved" work in the deployment guide), so Caddy's only job
#   here is HTTPS and passing everything through untouched. Websockets included
#   — reverse_proxy handles the upgrade without being told.
#
# RENAMING THIS FOR ANOTHER SITE: change the hostname below, and change the
# hosts-file line on that site's terminals to match. Nothing else.
# =============================================================================

pos-mississauga.local {
	tls internal
	encode gzip

	# `app` is the service name in docker-compose.prod.yml — Docker's internal
	# DNS resolves it to that container. Not 127.0.0.1: inside a container that
	# would mean Caddy itself.
	reverse_proxy app:4000
}
